//! The Thread module: spawning, joining, and the atomics that make sharing safe.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. W11's content, on the two language pieces it needed: a spellable `#c_call` procedure
//! type (ADR-0175) so a thread body can be handed to C, and atomics as real operations (ADR-0176) so two
//! threads can share a counter.
//!
//! # What a thread body must be
//!
//! A `#c_call` procedure taking a `*u8` and returning a `*u8`, because that is `pthread_create`'s signature
//! and this module is a binding rather than a runtime:
//!
//! ```text
//! worker :: (arg: *u8) -> *u8 #c_call {
//!     shared := typed(s64, arg);
//!     _ = atomic_add(shared, 1);
//!     return null;
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! **`#c_call` is not optional and not incidental.** An ordinary Jairs procedure takes the hidden context
//! parameter (ADR-0001), so C calling one would pass its `arg` where the context belongs and read the
//! context's field off the argument. The convention is what makes the two agree, and until ADR-0175 §1 it
//! could not be *written* in a type — which is why this module could not exist before.
//!
//! # What a thread body cannot do
//!
//! **It has no context**, so it cannot allocate: `context.allocator` is exactly what `#c_call` opts out of
//! (ADR-0057 §3). A worker that needs memory takes it through `arg`, which is the discipline C imposes
//! anyway and is why `arg` exists.
//!
//! **It cannot trap usefully.** The shadow call stack a backtrace walks is one module-wide object with one
//! depth counter (ADR-0066 §1), and two threads pushing onto it race. A trap in a spawned thread still
//! *stops the program* — the message may name the wrong frames. Stated rather than hidden: making the stack
//! per-thread needs thread-local storage in both back ends, which is its own wave (ADR-0177 §4).
//!
//! # Why there is no `Mutex`
//!
//! `pthread_mutex_t` is 64 bytes of opaque platform layout on macOS and a different size on Linux, and this
//! language has no way to say "an opaque N-byte thing whose N I looked up" without hard-coding N per
//! platform — the same wall `Socket`'s `sockaddr_in` hit (ADR-0158 §4) and lost more of.
//!
//! So this module offers the **spin lock** `acquire`/`release` built on `atomic_compare_exchange`, which
//! needs no platform layout at all and is correct. It burns CPU while contended, which is stated rather than
//! papered over: a caller holding a lock across a syscall wants a real mutex and should say so in an issue.
//!
//! # Why the atomics are not in this module
//!
//! `atomic_add` and friends are language intrinsics, not procedures here, because they must lower to machine
//! instructions in three engines and no library call can do that (ADR-0176 §1). They are usable without
//! importing this module at all, which is right: a program that shares one counter between two threads it
//! did not spawn still needs them.

libc :: #system_library "c";

/// `pthread_create`. The handle is written through `thread`.
///
/// `start` is spelled with its convention (ADR-0175 §1). Without that, a Jairs procedure could not be named
/// here at all: its type would be the context-taking one and no coercion exists between the two.
pthread_create :: (thread: *u8, attr: *u8, start: (*u8) -> *u8 #c_call, arg: *u8) -> s64 #foreign libc "pthread_create";

/// `pthread_join`. Takes the handle **by value**, not by pointer.
///
/// Spelled `*u8` because a `pthread_t` *is* a pointer on both supported platforms. Passing the address of the
/// handle instead compiles and returns `EINVAL` at run time, which is how this was got wrong first.
pthread_join :: (thread: *u8, result: *u8) -> s64 #foreign libc "pthread_join";

/// `sched_yield`, for a spin lock that has waited long enough to be embarrassing.
sched_yield :: () -> s64 #foreign libc "sched_yield";

/// A running thread.
///
/// A named one-field struct rather than a bare `*u8`, for `File`'s reason (ADR-0157 §1): a handle and a count
/// are both machine words, and the compiler will not confuse them once one has a name.
Thread :: struct {
    /// The `pthread_t`. Null before a successful spawn and after a join.
    handle: *u8;
}

/// The value an unlocked spin lock holds.
UNLOCKED :: 0;

/// The value a held spin lock holds.
LOCKED :: 1;

/// Starts `body` on a new thread, passing it `argument`.
///
/// `#must`: a spawn fails when the system is out of threads, and a caller who ignores that then *joins* a
/// null handle — which is `EINVAL` reported one step away from its cause, the failure chain the marker exists
/// for (ADR-0151).
///
/// `argument` is a `*u8` because C's is. A caller with a typed pointer passes `untyped(p)` and the body reads
/// it back with `typed(T, arg)`, which is the one erasing boundary ADR-0106 permits and names.
spawn :: (body: (*u8) -> *u8 #c_call, argument: *u8) -> (Thread, bool) #must {
    t: Thread;
    t.handle = null;
    if pthread_create(untyped(*t.handle), null, body, argument) != 0 {
        return t, false;
    }
    return t, t.handle != null;
}

/// Waits for `thread` to finish.
///
/// `#must`: joining twice, or joining a thread that never started, fails — and a caller who ignores it
/// continues as though the thread's writes were visible when they may not be, which is the one failure here
/// that produces a *wrong answer* rather than a stopped program.
///
/// Nulls the handle on success, so a second join is refused by this module rather than by libc: joining an
/// already-joined `pthread_t` is undefined behaviour in C, not an error, so catching it here is the
/// difference between a refusal and a crash.
join :: (thread: *Thread) -> bool #must {
    if thread.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    if pthread_join(thread.handle, null) != 0 {
        return false;
    }
    thread.handle = null;
    return true;
}

/// Whether `thread` is still ours to join.
///
/// Not "whether it is running": a thread may have finished and still be joinable, and reporting otherwise
/// would invite a caller to skip the join and leak it.
joinable :: (thread: *Thread) -> bool {
    return thread.handle != null;
}

/// Yields the processor, so a spinning thread lets a lock holder run.
yield_now :: () {
    _ = sched_yield();
}

/// Takes the spin lock at `lock`, waiting until it is free.
///
/// `lock` must point at an `s64` holding `UNLOCKED` or `LOCKED` and nothing else — it is not a struct,
/// because a one-field struct would need `typed`/`untyped` at every call and buy nothing.
///
/// **A spin lock, not a mutex**, for the reason in the module docs: `pthread_mutex_t`'s layout is opaque and
/// platform-sized. This burns CPU while contended and yields on every failed attempt, which is what keeps a
/// single-core machine from live-locking — a bare spin with no yield deadlocks there whenever the holder is
/// descheduled.
///
/// Not `#must`: it does not fail, it waits.
acquire :: (lock: *s64) {
    while !atomic_compare_exchange(lock, UNLOCKED, LOCKED) {
        yield_now();
    }
}

/// Releases the spin lock at `lock`.
///
/// An atomic **store** rather than a compare-exchange: only the holder releases, so there is nothing to
/// check, and a store is the cheaper of the two. A caller releasing a lock it does not hold is a bug this
/// cannot detect — recording that plainly, because the alternative is an owner field and a thread identity
/// this module does not have.
release :: (lock: *s64) {
    atomic_store(lock, UNLOCKED);
}

/// Whether the lock at `lock` is currently held.
///
/// For a diagnostic, never for a decision: by the time a caller reads the answer it may be stale, which is
/// the whole difficulty of shared state. `acquire` is the only correct way to act on it.
is_locked :: (lock: *s64) -> bool {
    return atomic_load(lock) == LOCKED;
}
